course: dictionary
lesson: 15582
title: Sworn: one word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words look the same but mean different things in different sentences. Context tells you which meaning to use. Sworn comes from the verb swear, and it follows have or has: «I have sworn to keep the secret.»
explain: «The witness was sworn in before she told the court what she saw.» Sworn in means she made a serious promise to tell the truth. The court and the word witness are the clues. Ask: what would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: «Tom and Ben are sworn enemies, and they never speak in class.» Here sworn shows that the enemies are completely and permanently against each other. Compare «He has sworn at the referee», where sworn comes from swear meaning to use rude words. The rest of the sentence tells you which meaning is right.
fact: Sworn is the past participle of swear: swear, swears, swore, sworn, swearing.
fact: «Sworn» can mean made with a serious promise, as in «a sworn statement».
fact: «Sworn enemies» or «sworn friends» means completely and permanently enemies or friends.
fact: «Sworn to secrecy» means promised not to tell.
fact: «Sworn» can also come from swear meaning to use rude words, as in «He has sworn at the referee».
exercise: «I have sworn to keep the secret.» What does sworn show here? | A rude word | A loud shout | *A serious promise | A lost key
exercise: «The witness was sworn in before the trial began.» What does sworn in mean? | They were cross with the judge | They were given a cup of tea | They ran out of the court | *They promised to tell the truth
exercise: «Lily and Ruby are sworn enemies.» What does sworn enemies mean? | Children who sing very loudly | Enemies who have lost a game | Friends who share a secret | *Enemies who are completely and permanently against each other
test: «The referee said the player had sworn at him.» What does sworn mean here? | To have made a serious promise | To have run very fast | To have been given a prize | *To have used rude words
test: Which phrase means promised not to tell? | Sworn statement | Sworn enemies | Sworn in | *Sworn to secrecy
exam: «The sworn statement was read aloud in court.» What does sworn mean? | Written in red ink | Very old and dusty | Made by a friend as a joke | *Given after promising to tell the truth
